---
import ComparisonTable from "@components/article/ComparisonTable.astro";
import FAQSection from "@components/article/FAQSection.astro";
import RelatedArticles from "@components/article/RelatedArticles.astro";
import ArticleLayout from "../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro";

// Article metadata
const title = "Cloud vs Traditional Hosting: What Actually Matters (2025)";
const description =
  "I've migrated sites at 3am while clients panicked about downtime. Don't be me. Here's how to pick the right hosting type the first time and avoid disasters.";
const author = "FloatJet Team";
const authorUrl = "/about";
const pubDate = new Date("2025-11-23");
const readingTime = 4;
const image = "/images/unsplash/photo-1451187580459-43490279c0fa-1200w.webp";
const imageAlt = "Cloud computing concept with server infrastructure visualization";

// Table of contents
const headings = [
  { text: "The Real Question", slug: "introduction", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Traditional Hosting", slug: "traditional-hosting", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Cloud Hosting", slug: "cloud-hosting", depth: 2 },
  { text: "The Actual Difference", slug: "key-differences", depth: 2 },
  { text: "So Which One?", slug: "which-to-choose", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Who I'd Actually Use", slug: "best-providers", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Quick Answers", slug: "faq", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Bottom Line", slug: "conclusion", depth: 2 },
];

// Comparison table
const comparisonColumns = [
  { key: "feature", label: "Feature", width: "140px" },
  { key: "traditional", label: "Traditional" },
  { key: "cloud", label: "Cloud" },
];

const comparisonRows = [
  {
    feature: "<strong>Scalability</strong>",
    traditional: "Limited - must upgrade plan",
    cloud: "Instant - scale on demand",
  },
  {
    feature: "<strong>Pricing</strong>",
    traditional: "Fixed monthly cost",
    cloud: "Pay for what you use",
  },
  {
    feature: "<strong>Uptime</strong>",
    traditional: "99.9% typical",
    cloud: "99.99%+ with redundancy",
  },
  {
    feature: "<strong>Performance</strong>",
    traditional: "Shared resources",
    cloud: "Dedicated resources",
  },
  {
    feature: "<strong>Setup</strong>",
    traditional: "Simple, beginner-friendly",
    cloud: "More technical knowledge needed",
  },
  {
    feature: "<strong>Best For</strong>",
    traditional: "Small sites, blogs",
    cloud: "Growing businesses, apps",
  },
];

// FAQ data
const faqs = [
  {
    question: "Cloud costs more?",
    answer:
      "<p>Depends. Spiky traffic? Cloud might be cheaper. Steady traffic? Traditional wins.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "Can I switch later?",
    answer:
      "<p>Yeah. Most hosts help with migration. Back up, transfer, update DNS. Not fun but doable.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "Need to be technical for cloud?",
    answer: "<p>Not with managed hosts. Cloudways and Kinsta handle the hard stuff.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "Better for WordPress?",
    answer:
      '<p>Small blog? Traditional shared is fine. Business site? <a href="/blog/managed-wordpress-hosting-compared">Managed WordPress cloud</a>. WP Engine or Kinsta.</p>',
  },
  {
    question: "Traffic spike on traditional hosting?",
    answer: "<p>Site slows or crashes. On cloud? Scales automatically. You just pay more.</p>",
  },
];

// Related articles
const relatedArticles = [
  {
    title: "Best Web Hosting for Remote Businesses",
    excerpt: "Complete comparison of top hosting providers for remote teams and online businesses.",
    href: "/tools/best-web-hosting-remote-business",
    image: "/images/unsplash/photo-1558494949-ef010cbdcc31-800w.webp",
    imageAlt: "Server room with hosting infrastructure",
  },
  {
    title: "Managed WordPress Hosting Compared",
    excerpt: "Find the best managed WordPress host for your blog or business site.",
    href: "/blog/managed-wordpress-hosting-compared",
    image: "/images/unsplash/photo-1504639725590-34d0984388bd-800w.webp",
    imageAlt: "WordPress development on laptop",
  },
  {
    title: "Best Project Management Software",
    excerpt: "Organize your remote business with the right project management tools.",
    href: "/tools/best-project-management-software",
    image: "/images/unsplash/photo-1454165804606-c3d57bc86b40-800w.webp",
    imageAlt: "Project management dashboard",
  },
];
---

<ArticleLayout
    title={title}
    description={description}
    author={author}
    authorUrl={authorUrl}
    pubDate={pubDate}
    readingTime={readingTime}
    image={image}
    imageAlt={imageAlt}
    category="Hosting"
    categorySlug="tools/hosting"
    headings={headings}
>
  <!-- Introduction -->
  <h2 id="introduction">The Real Question</h2>
  <p>
    Site goes viral. Traffic spikes. Then... nothing. White screen of death.
  </p>
  <p>
    Been there. 3am. Client's <a href="/tools/best-ecommerce-hosting">e-commerce site</a> down during a flash sale.
    Shared hosting couldn't handle it.
    I'm on the phone with support, they're telling me to "upgrade my plan." Cool. Thanks. Super helpful
    while orders are bouncing.
  </p>
  <p>
    That night cost my client thousands. And me a lot of sleep.
  </p>
  <p>
    Here's the thing though—cloud hosting wouldn't have been right for them six months earlier when
    they were getting 50 visitors a day. Context matters. Let me break this down.
  </p>

  <!-- Traditional Hosting -->
  <h2 id="traditional-hosting">Traditional Hosting</h2>
  <p>
    One physical server. Your site lives there. Fixed monthly cost.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Shared hosting</strong> is the cheapest. Like $3-15/month cheap. But you're splitting
    resources with hundreds of other sites. Someone else's traffic spike? Your problem now.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>VPS</strong> gives you dedicated resources on a shared machine. $20-80/month.
    Actually usable for real businesses.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Dedicated servers</strong> are the whole machine, just for you. $80-300+. Overkill for most people.
  </p>

  <h3>Why I still recommend it for beginners:</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>You know exactly what you'll pay. No surprises.</li>
    <li>Setup takes like... 10 minutes? Most have one-click <a
        href="/blog/managed-wordpress-hosting-compared">WordPress</a>.
    </li>
    <li>Five bucks a month to start. Hard to beat that.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>The tradeoffs:</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Need more resources? That means migrating. During downtime. Fun.</li>
    <li>Server has issues? Your site has issues.</li>
    <li>"Unlimited bandwidth" is a lie. Read the terms.</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- Cloud Hosting -->
  <h2 id="cloud-hosting">Cloud Hosting</h2>
  <p>
    Different concept entirely.
  </p>
  <p>
    Instead of one server, your site pulls from a network of them. Need more power during a traffic
    spike? It's just... there. Traffic dies down? You stop paying for the extra.
  </p>
  <p>
    Think of it like electricity. You don't run your own generator. You plug into the grid and pay
    for what you use.
  </p>

  <h3>Why it saved me (eventually):</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>That client I mentioned? Moved them to cloud. Next sale went fine. Zero intervention.</li>
    <li>One server failing doesn't matter. Others pick up the slack.</li>
    <li>Scaling happens automatically. No 3am phone calls.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>What's annoying:</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Your bill changes every month. Viral post? Surprise invoice.</li>
    <li>Raw AWS or Google Cloud? Steep learning curve. Like, really steep.</li>
    <li>Gotta watch your usage or costs spiral fast.</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- Key Differences -->
  <h2 id="key-differences">The Actual Difference</h2>

  <ComparisonTable columns={comparisonColumns} rows={comparisonRows}/>

  <p>
    <strong>Traditional:</strong> Fixed cost. Simpler. 99.9% uptime if you're lucky. Great for small
    sites that don't change much.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Cloud:</strong> Variable cost. More complex. 99.99%+ uptime with redundancy. Built for
    things that need to grow.
  </p>

  <!-- Which Should You Choose? -->
  <h2 id="which-to-choose">So Which One?</h2>
  <p>
    Honestly? Most people starting out should go traditional.
  </p>

  <h3>Pick traditional if you're:</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Running a <a href="/guides/productivity">blog or portfolio</a></li>
    <li>Getting steady, predictable traffic</li>
    <li>Watching every dollar</li>
    <li>Not interested in server management</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Pick cloud if you're:</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/guides/productivity">Building something</a> that might actually take off</li>
    <li>Traffic is spiky or growing fast</li>
    <li>Downtime means lost money</li>
    <li>Willing to pay for peace of mind</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    <strong>The cheat code:</strong> Managed cloud hosting. Cloudways, Kinsta, WP Engine. Cloud
    performance, traditional simplicity. They handle the technical nightmares so you don't have to.
  </p>

  <!-- Best Providers -->
  <h2 id="best-providers">Who I'd Actually Use</h2>

  <h3>For traditional:</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>SiteGround</strong> - Support is actually helpful. WordPress runs smooth. From $2.99/month.</li>
    <li><strong>Bluehost</strong> - WordPress recommends them. Beginner-proof. From $2.95/month.</li>
    <li><strong>A2 Hosting</strong> - Fast servers. Devs like it. From $2.99/month.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>For cloud:</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Cloudways</strong> - Best value. Pick your cloud (AWS, Google, DigitalOcean). From $14/month.
      <a href="/go/cloudways" rel="nofollow sponsored">Try Cloudways &rarr;</a>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Kinsta</strong> - Premium. Google Cloud underneath. From $35/month.
      <a href="/go/kinsta" rel="nofollow sponsored">Try Kinsta &rarr;</a>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>WP Engine</strong> - The "I don't want to think about hosting" option. From $20/month.
      <a href="/go/wpengine" rel="nofollow sponsored">Try WP Engine &rarr;</a>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    For detailed reviews, see our <a href="/tools/best-web-hosting-remote-business">complete hosting comparison</a>.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ Section -->
  <FAQSection faqs={faqs}/>

  <!-- Conclusion -->
  <h2 id="conclusion">Bottom Line</h2>
  <p>
    Starting out with a blog or small site? SiteGround. Traditional shared hosting. Simple, cheap, works.
  </p>
  <p>
    Building something real? Cloudways for value. Kinsta or WP Engine if you want premium.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href="/tools/migrate-website-without-downtime">Migration</a> isn't that painful anymore. Start with what fits
    your budget now. Upgrade when you
    actually need to.
  </p>
  <p>
    Just... don't wait until 3am during a client's flash sale to figure this out. Learn from my mistakes.
  </p>

  <!-- Related Articles -->
  <Fragment slot="full-width">
    <RelatedArticles articles={relatedArticles}/>
  </Fragment>
</ArticleLayout>
